    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures).\n\n## What I did\n\nMoved the owner's date-derived values onto a **fiscal-year basis** (fiscal year starts 1 July, named by the calendar year it ends in \u2014 the Australian convention that matches this codebase's AU data). Concretely:\n\n1. **New `FiscalYear` helper** (`rest/function/owner/FiscalYear.java`) \u2014 a single source of truth for fiscal-year math (`endingYear`, `twoDigit`, `label`, `elapsed`, `forOwner`), sitting alongside the existing `OwnerLocality`/`OwnerIdentityKey`/`TelephoneDisplay` helpers.\n\n2. **`fiscalYear` field** \u2014 added to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml` (so the generated `OwnerDto` gets it) and wired in `OwnerMapper` as `FY<YY>` derived from the business-day-adjusted registration date. This satisfies the new `coreReturnsFiscalYear` test (`FY\\d{2}`).\n\n3. **Membership identifier year segment** \u2014 switched from calendar year (`getYear() % 100`) to the fiscal year, in **both** places it was computed: `OwnerMapper` (the API response) and `AuditOwnerCreated` (the audit log). A registration dated today now reads `-M27` instead of `-M26`.\n\n4. **Tenure** \u2014 `Owner.getMembershipPoints()` now awards its 3 points when at least one whole fiscal year has elapsed since registration, replacing the old `> 365`-day window. I inlined the fiscal math here rather than reference the REST-layer helper, keeping the model layer dependency-free.\n\n5. **Docs** \u2014 updated the `membershipNumber`, `membershipPoints`, and new `fiscalYear` descriptions in `openapi.yml` and the Javadoc in `Owner.java` to match the new behaviour.\n\n### One judgement call worth flagging\nThe spec says `FY<YY>` \"where the fiscal year starts on 1 July\" but doesn't state whether `<YY>` is the fiscal year's **starting** or **ending** calendar year. I chose the **ending** year (1 Jul 2025\u201330 Jun 2026 \u2192 `FY26`) because it's the standard Australian financial-year abbreviation and it's the reading that makes the highlighted behaviour change visible for current-dated registrations. The provided test only checks the `FY\\d{2}` format, so it passes either way \u2014 if your intended convention is the *starting* year, it's a one-line change in `FiscalYear.endingYear` / `Owner.fiscalEndingYear`; let me know and I'll flip it.",
